language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ five_brigands: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The five Spanish brigands, Alfonso, Benito, Carlos, Diego and Esteban, were counting their spoils after a raid, when it was found that they had captured altogether exactly 200 doubloons. One of the band pointed out that if Alfonso had twelve times as much, Benito three times as much, Carlos the same amount, Diego half as much, and Esteban one-third as much, they would still have altogether just 200 doubloons. How many doubloons had each? Also, no brigand had less than one doubloon. Give the number of doubloons that each brigand may have (A, B, C, D, E).
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/five_brigands.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ the total number of doubloons
letting total = 200
$ A, B, C, D, E: the doubloons held by Alfonso, Benito, Carlos, Diego and Esteban; each is at least one and at most the total of 200
find A : int(1..total)
find B : int(1..total)
find C : int(1..total)
find D : int(1..total)
find E : int(1..total)

such that
$ together the brigands hold exactly 200 doubloons
A + B + C + D + E = total,
$ with Alfonso at twelve times, Benito at three times, Carlos the same, Diego at half and Esteban at one-third as much, the total is still 200 (both sides multiplied by 6 to keep the coefficients whole)
72 * A + 18 * B + 6 * C + 3 * D + 2 * E = 6 * total
